 has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-xian-of-jin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/duke-xian-of-jin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Duke Xian of Jin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Duke Xian of Jin was ruler of the state of Jin in northern China during the Spring and Autumn period.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-xian-of-qin"><span class="enc-name">Duke Xian of Qin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Duke Xian of Qin ruled the State of Qin from 384 to 362 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-xiang-of-qin"><span class="enc-name">Duke Xiang of Qin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Duke Xiang of Qin ruled the Qin people from 777 to 766 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-xiang-of-song"><span class="enc-name">Duke Xiang of Song</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Duke Xiang of Song ruled the State of Song from 650 to 637 BCE and sought to become hegemon of the Zhou states.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-xiao-of-qin"><span class="enc-name">Duke Xiao of Qin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Duke Xiao of Qin ruled the State of Qin from 361 to 338 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-zhuang-of-zheng"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/duke-zhuang-of-zheng.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Duke Zhuang of Zheng</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Duke Zhuang of Zheng (reigned 743–701 BCE) was a ruler of the State of Zheng who made it the leading state of the early Spring and Autumn period.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dukes-chair"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dukes-chair.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Duke's Chair</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Duke's Chair is a double stone throne on the Zollfeld plain north of Klagenfurt in Carinthia, Austria, probably assembled in the 9th century from reused Roman stones.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dukes-laws"><span class="enc-name">Duke's Laws</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Duke's Laws were the legal code issued in 1665 for the territories of James, Duke of York, after the English capture of New Netherland.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dukhan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dukhan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dukhan</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dukhan is a town on the west coast of Qatar and the centre of the country's main onshore oil field.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dukla-pass"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dukla-pass.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dukla Pass</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Dukla Pass is a low pass in the Carpathian Mountains on the border between Poland and Slovakia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dukuza"><span class="enc-name">Dukuza</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dukuza was the royal capital built by the Zulu king Shaka around 1827 near the coast north of Port Natal, in what is now KwaZulu-Natal.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dulat-tribe"><span class="enc-name">Dulat (tribe)</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Dulat are one of the largest tribes of the Senior Jüz of the K